.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1200 PM PST Tue Nov 24 2026
(This evening through Wednesday night)

In the short term, the primary focus remains on impacts from the
King Tides, particularly late Wednesday morning and around noon on
Thanksgiving, at peak high tide. As these tides peak to historic
levels, low-lying areas that have seen coastal flooding in recent
weeks will continue to see flooding, with inundation for areas up
to a foot higher than yesterday`s high tide mark also at risk.
See more details on the coastal flooding below in the beaches
section.

This morning, satellite imagery shows a 200-300 mile cloud field
associated with a cold front beginning to move through the region.
Increasing cloud cover is expected today from the northwest with
showers beginning to develop along the northwest coast of Sonoma
County early this afternoon, leading way to a modest rain band
sweeping through the region tonight, with lingering showers
Wednesday morning.

The main cold front moves through the region tonight, delivering
south wind gusts 25-35 mph for coastal and bay shore areas this
evening and most of the rainfall during the overnight hours.
While radar returns will initially look quite impressive off the
north coast this evening the associated trough pushing the front
will be rapidly lifting north. The front will lose a significant
amount of energy causing the rain band to steadily weaken as it
pushes south and inland through the Bay Area. By early into
Wednesday`s commute, the rain band will have broken apart into
scattered showers for the inland East Bay, Santa Cruz mountains
and Monterey county coast, fizzling out completely by the
afternoon. The remaining cumulus clouds over the region will
progressively become shallower and thin out, yielding to some
afternoon sunshine.

As a result, rainfall totals will taper off sharply from north to
south and from coast to inland. Modest accumulations are expected
across Marin and Sonoma Counties (~0.50 - 1.50", highest north
and along terrain) as well as along the coastal slopes of the
Peninsula down to Santa Cruz (0.5-0.75"). San Francisco and areas
along the bay shore are expected to see 0.25-0.50". The East Bay
interior valleys, the South Bay and Monterey County coast are
expected light totals, generally less than 0.25" with the
southernmost and farthest inland valleys receiving less than
0.10", likely remaining completely dry south of Salinas.

While the majority of the rainfall will be light, weak surface-
based instability (200-400 J/kg) over the coastal waters could
bring coastal areas north of Santa Cruz a few heavier showers or
a thunderstorm (~15% chance). Both the HRRR and the RRFS are in
agreement in localized rain rates over 0.50"/hr, with the HRRR
hinting at the possibility of a shower near 1"/hr. Any heavier
downpours could lead to localized flooding.

Wednesday and Thursday nights, calm winds will allow for patchy
fog formations across the inland valleys, although high clouds may
inhibit its coverage.

